First`, `< Prev`, `Play / Pause`, `Next >`, `Last >>|`, timeline scrubber, and speed selector (`0.5x` to `4x`).\n  - Preloaded beginner curriculum examples (*For Loops*, *Variables & Arithmetic*, *If/Elif/Else Conditionals*, *Find Max Algorithm*, *While Loops*, and *Functions & Call Stack*).\n- **Native Python `sys.settrace` Server ([server.py](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/server.py))**:\n  - Optional zero-dependency Python backend (`/api/trace`) using `sys.settrace`. You can also open [index.html](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/index.html) directly in your browser.\n\n---\n\n**Ready for Step 2!** Please share your next step/requirements, and I will implement them right away.","thinking":"Project initialization is complete. The working directory is set to facilitate subsequent operations on the code flow visualizer project.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":15,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:03:53Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n2. Main Goal\n\nI am a beginner programmer. When someone gives me a Python program, I want to understand:\n\nWhat happens when the code starts executing?\nWhich line executes first, second, third, and next?\nWhat does each variable contain at every step?\nHow do loops work internally?\nHow do conditions evaluate to True or False?\nHow does the program decide which line to execute next?\nHow do functions, parameters, arguments, and return statements work?\nHow does the final answer get calculated?\nWhy does the code work this way?\n\nThe application should teach me as if a patient senior developer is sitting next to me and explaining every line.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-10-10T15:33:53+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":16,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:03:53Z","input_tokens":3686,"cache_read_tokens":48921,"output_tokens":15718,"thinking":"The primary objective is to grasp Python program execution flow. Understanding the initial execution point and subsequent line-by-line progression is crucial. Also, essential to track variable states at each step, especially during loop iterations.\n\n\n**Step 0: Program Startup:**\n\nThe program begins execution at a predetermined entry point. This point is often the first line of the main script or a designated \"main\" function. From there, the interpreter proceeds sequentially unless control flow statements alter the execution path.\n\n**Program Execution Journey & Order Tracker:**\n\nEach line of code executes in a specific order. Execution order is usually sequential, but control flow statements like `if/else`, `for`, and `while` loops direct the program to different lines based on conditions. The execution order is tracked with line numbers next to each line in the UI, enabling a clear visual journey.\n\n**Why This Line Next? (Control Flow Decision):**\n\nThe program decides which line to execute next by evaluating conditions and following the logic defined by control flow statements. For example, an `if` statement evaluates a condition (e.g., `x > 5`). If the condition is True, the code inside the `if` block executes; otherwise, the `else` block (if present) or the next line after the `if/else` block executes. Loops iterate over a block of code repeatedly as long as a specified condition is True.\n\n\nUpon startup, the interpreter creates a global memory frame, and the instruction pointer is set to the beginning of the code (Line 1). Code execution proceeds top-to-bottom unless control flow statements alter the path. Loops require specific internal tracking to understand the internal processing of each iterator.\n\nLoop internals are visualized to clarify operation. The visualization includes the loop type, current iteration, and iterable/condition. For `for` loops, this includes the iterable with a current element pointer. `While` loops visualize the condition check's evaluation at each iteration to explain the control flow.\n\n\nExecution begins, initializing the program's memory frame. Line-by-line progression will be tracked, noting changes to variables. Control flow statements will dictate the execution path.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"// AI-Powered Code Flow Visualizer - Python Execution Tracer & Patient Senior Dev Mentor Engine\n\nconst PRESET_EXAMPLES = {\n  for_loop_sum: {\n    title: \"1. For Loop: Sum of Even Numbers\",\n    concept: \"Loops, Lists & Conditionals\",\n    bigPicture: \"This program walks through a list of numbers one by one, checks if each number is even (divisible by 2 with remainder 0), and keeps a running total (`even_sum`) and count (`count`) of only the even numbers.\",\n    code: `numbers = [4, 7, 10, 3, 8]\neven_sum = 0\ncount = 0\n\nfor num in numbers:\n    if num % 2 == 0:\n        even_sum = even_sum + num\n        count = count + 1\n\nprint(\"Even count:\", count)\nprint(\"Even sum:\", even_sum)`\n  },\n  variables_swap: {\n    title: \"2. Variables & Memory: Price & Discount Calculator\",\n    concept: \"Variables, Arithmetic & Sequential Flow\",\n    bigPicture: \"This program demonstrates sequential top-to-bottom execution: it stores initial numbers in variables, then computes new values step by step by reading existing variables from memory.\",\n    code: `price = 120\ntax_rate = 10\ndiscount = 25\n\ndiscounted = price - discount\ntax_amount = (discounted * tax_rate) // 100\nfinal_total = discounted + tax_amount\n\nprint(\"Final Total:\", final_total)`\n  },\n  conditionals_grade: {\n    title: \"3. Conditionals: Student Grade Classifier\",\n    concept: \"if / elif / else Branching Decisions\",\n    bigPicture: \"This program calculates a student's final score and uses an `if / elif / else` decision ladder. Python checks each condition from top to bottom, runs ONLY the first branch that evaluates to True, and skips the rest.\",\n    code: `score = 84\nbonus = 5\nfinal_score = score + bonus\n\nif final_score >= 90:\n    grade = \"A\"\nelif final_score >= 80:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelif final_score >= 70:\n    grade = \"C\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"F\"\n\nprint(\"Score:\", final_score)\nprint(\"Grade:\", grade)`\n  },\n  find_max_algorithm: {\n    title: \"4. Algorithm: Find Maximum in a List\",\n    concept: \"List Indexing & State Tracking\",\n    bigPicture: \"To find the largest number in a list, we start by assuming the first item (`scores[0]`) is the champion (`max_score`). Then we loop through the remaining indices and update `max_score` whenever we see a bigger number.\",\n    code: `scores = [18, 42, 29, 55, 31]\nmax_score = scores[0]\nbest_index = 0\n\nfor i in range(1, 5):\n    current = scores[i]\n    if current > max_score:\n        max_score = current\n        best_index = i\n\nprint(\"Highest score:\", max_score)\nprint(\"Found at index:\", best_index)`\n  },\n  while_countdown: {\n    title: \"5. While Loop: Doubling Until Target\",\n    concept: \"While Condition & Loop State\",\n    bigPicture: \"A `while` loop repeats when we don't know in advance how many iterations we need. Before every pass, Python tests `value < target`. As soon as doubling `value` makes it 40 or more, the condition becomes False and the loop stops.\",\n    code: `value = 3\ntarget = 40\nsteps = 0\n\nwhile value < target:\n    value = value * 2\n    steps = steps + 1\n    print(\"Step\", steps, \"-> value is\", value)\n\nprint(\"Reached\", value, \"in\", steps, \"steps\")`\n  },\n  functions_factorial: {\n    title: \"6. Functions & Call Stack: Computing Factorial\",\n    concept: \"Functions, Parameters & Return Values\",\n    bigPicture: \"This program shows how functions work: `def` teaches Python a reusable recipe (`multiply_step`). Inside the loop, calling `multiply_step(answer, k)` passes arguments into parameters, runs the function in its own Call Stack frame, and `return` sends the answer back.\",\n    code: `def multiply_step(current, factor):\n    result = current * factor\n    return result\n\nn = 4\nanswer = 1\n\nfor k in range(1, n + 1):\n    answer = multiply_step(answer, k)\n\nprint(\"Factorial of\", n, \"is\", answer)`\n  }\n};\n\nclass PythonFlowTracer {\n  constructor() {\n    this.maxSteps = 250;\n  }\n\n  trace(sourceCode, bigPictureNote = \"\") {\n    const rawLines = sourceCode.replace(/\\r\\n/g, \"\\n\").split(\"\\n\");\n    const statements = this.parseLines(rawLines);\n    const cfg = this.buildControlFlowGraph(statements);\n\n    const steps = [];\n    const stdout = [];\n    const functions = {};\n    const callStack = [\n      {\n        frameName: \"Global Frame (main)\",\n        vars: {},\n        prevVars: {}\n      }\n    ];\n\n    // Step 0 / Startup Step: Explain what happens when the code starts executing!\n    const firstExecutableLine = statements.length > 0 ? statements[0].line : 1;\n    const firstCodeText = rawLines[firstExecutableLine - 1] ? rawLines[firstExecutableLine - 1].trim() : \"\";\n    steps.push({\n      stepIndex: 1,\n      line: firstExecutableLine,\n      nextLine: firstExecutableLine,\n      codeText: firstCodeText,\n      eventType: \"PROGRAM_START\",\n      scopes: this.snapshotScopes(callStack, null),\n      evalBreakdown: `Program Startup -> Creating Global Memory Frame & placing Instruction Pointer at Line ${firstExecutableLine}`,\n      branchResult: null,\n      loopMechanics: null,\n      conditionMechanics: null,\n      functionMechanics: null,\n      stdout: [],\n      explanations: {\n        eli5: `Welcome! Before running any math or logic, Python sets up an empty desk called the **Global Frame** to hold your variables, and points its finger at **Line ${firstExecutableLine}** to begin reading from top to bottom.`,\n        beginner: `**Program Startup**: Python initializes the \\`Global Frame (main)\\` in memory (currently empty) and positions the execution pointer at the first executable statement on **Line ${firstExecutableLine}** (\\`${firstCodeText}\\`).`,\n        deep: `Interpreter initializes the module-level global namespace dictionary and sets the frame instruction pointer to the first AST statement at line ${firstExecutableLine}.`,\n        whyItHappened: `Python always starts executing a script from the top-most statement (skipping blank lines and comments) and works downward unless a loop, branch, or function call redirects it.`,\n        howNextLineDecided: `The program starts at **Line ${firstExecutableLine}** because it is the very first instruction in the file.`,\n        seniorMentorNote: bigPictureNote || `Let's walk through this program together line by line. Watch how each line updates the variables in memory and how Python decides where to go next!`\n      },\n      quiz: null\n    });\n\n    try {\n      this.executeBlock(statements, callStack, functions, steps, stdout);\n    } catch (err) {\n      steps.push({\n        stepIndex: steps.length + 1,\n        line: err.line || 1,\n        nextLine: null,\n        codeText: rawLines[(err.line || 1) - 1] || \"\",\n        eventType: \"ERROR\",\n        scopes: this.snapshotScopes(callStack, null),\n        evalBreakdown: `Runtime Error: ${err.message}`,\n        branchResult: null,\n        loopMechanics: null,\n        conditionMechanics: null,\n        functionMechanics: null,\n        stdout: [...stdout],\n        explanations: {\n          eli5: `Oops! Python ran into an error on line ${err.line || 1}: ${err.message}`,\n          beginner: `Execution stopped on line ${err.line || 1} because of: ${err.message}`,\n          deep: `Exception raised during statement evaluation: ${err.message}`,\n          whyItHappened: `Check syntax or variable names on line ${err.line || 1}.`,\n          howNextLineDecided: `Execution halted due to an unhandled error.`,\n          whatNext: `Execution halted due to error.`\n        },\n        quiz: null\n      });\n    }\n\n    // Post-process steps to populate exact Next Line Decision explanations, Execution Order history, and Quizzes\n    for (let i = 0; i < steps.length; i++) {\n      const cur = steps[i];\n      const nextStep = i + 1 < steps.length ? steps[i + 1] : null;\n      cur.nextLine = nextStep ? nextStep.line : null;\n\n      if (!cur.explanations.howNextLineDecided) {\n        cur.explanations.howNextLineDecided = this.explainNextLineDecision(cur, nextStep);\n      }\n\n      cur.explanations.whatNext = nextStep\n        ? `Next (**Step ${nextStep.stepIndex}**), Python moves to **Line ${nextStep.line}** (\\`${nextStep.codeText.trim()}\\`) to ${this.describeUpcomingAction(nextStep)}.`\n        : `🎉 **Program Complete!** All instructions have finished executing. Check the **Final Answer & Variable Timeline** tab to see how every variable evolved from start to finish!`;\n\n      cur.quiz = this.generateQuizForStep(cur, nextStep);\n    }\n\n    const variableTimeline = this.buildVariableTimeline(steps);\n\n    return {\n      rawLines,\n      steps,\n      cfg,\n      variableTimeline\n    };\n  }\n\n  explainNextLineDecision(cur, nextStep) {\n    if (!nextStep) {\n      return `There are no more statements left in the program, so Python finishes execution.`;\n    }\n    if (cur.eventType === \"PROGRAM_START\") {\n      return `Python begins by executing **Line ${nextStep.line}** first because code runs top-to-bottom.`;\n    }\n    if (cur.eventType === \"CONDITION\") {\n      if (cur.branchResult === true) {\n        return `Because the condition on Line ${cur.line} evaluated to **True**, Python steps **inside** the indented block at **Line ${nextStep.line}**.`;\n      } else {\n        return `Because the condition on Line ${cur.line} evaluated to **False**, Python **skips** the indented block and jumps directly to **Line ${nextStep.line}**.`;\n      }\n    }\n    if (cur.eventType === \"LOOP_ITER\") {\n      return `The loop is active, so Python enters the indented loop body starting at **Line ${nextStep.line}**.`;\n    }\n    if (cur.eventType === \"LOOP_END\") {\n      return `The loop has finished all iterations, so Python jumps past the loop body to the next statement after the loop (**Line ${nextStep.line}**).`;\n    }\n    if (cur.eventType === \"FUNC_CALL\") {\n      return `A function was called! Python pauses the caller line and jumps to the first line inside the function body (**Line ${nextStep.line}**).`;\n    }\n    if (cur.eventType === \"RETURN\") {\n      return `\\`return\\` hands the result back to the caller, so Python jumps back to **Line ${nextStep.line}** where the function was called.`;\n    }\n    if (nextStep.line < cur.line) {\n      return `We reached the bottom of the loop body on Line ${cur.line}, so Python **loops back up** to the loop header on **Line ${nextStep.line}** for the next check!`;\n    }\n    return `Normal sequential flow: after finishing Line ${cur.line}, Python moves straight down to the next statement on **Line ${nextStep.line}**.`;\n  }\n\n  buildVariableTimeline(steps) {\n    const history = [];\n    steps.forEach((s) => {\n      const activeScope = s.scopes[s.scopes.length - 1];\n      if (!activeScope) return;\n      activeScope.variables.forEach((v) => {\n        if (v.status === \"new\" || v.status === \"updated\") {\n          history.push({\n            stepIndex: s.stepIndex,\n            line: s.line,\n            frameName: activeScope.frameName,\n            varName: v.name,\n            prevVal: v.prevFormattedValue,\n            newVal: v.formattedValue,\n            status: v.status,\n            codeText: s.codeText,\n            evalBreakdown: s.evalBreakdown\n          });\n        }\n      });\n    });\n    return history;\n  }\n\n  parseLines(rawLines) {\n    const items = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < rawLines.length; i++) {\n      const raw = rawLines[i];\n      const strippedComment = this.stripInlineComment(raw);\n      const trimmed = strippedComment.trim();\n      if (!trimmed) continue;\n      const indent = raw.match(/^\\s*/)[0].length;\n      items.push({\n        line: i + 1,\n        indent,\n        text: trimmed,\n        raw: raw\n      });\n    }\n    return this.buildASTBlock(items, 0, items.length > 0 ? items[0].indent : 0).nodes;\n  }\n\n  stripInlineComment(line) {\n    let inSingle = false;\n    let inDouble = false;\n    for (let i = 0; i < line.length; i++) {\n      const ch = line[i];\n      if (ch === \"'\" && !inDouble) inSingle = !inSingle;\n      else if (ch === '\"' && !inSingle) inDouble = !inDouble;\n      else if (ch === \"#\" && !inSingle && !inDouble) {\n        return line.slice(0, i);\n      }\n    }\n    return line;\n  }\n\n  buildASTBlock(items, startIdx, baseIndent) {\n    const nodes = [];\n    let i = startIdx;\n\n    while (i < items.length) {\n      const item = items[i];\n      if (item.indent < baseIndent) break;\n\n      if (item.text.startsWith(\"if \") && item.text.endsWith(\":\")) {\n        const branches = [];\n        const cond = item.text.slice(3, -1).trim();\n        const bodyRes = this.parseChildSuite(items, i + 1, item.indent);\n        branches.push({\n          type: \"if\",\n          line: item.line,\n          text: item.text,\n          condition: cond,\n          body: bodyRes.nodes\n        });\n        i = bodyRes.nextIdx;\n\n        while (i < items.length && items[i].indent === item.indent) {\n          const cur = items[i];\n          if (cur.text.startsWith(\"elif \") && cur.text.endsWith(\":\")) {\n            const elifCond = cur.text.slice(5, -1).trim();\n            const elifBody = this.parseChildSuite(items, i + 1, cur.indent);\n            branches.push({\n              type: \"elif\",\n              line: cur.line,\n              text: cur.text,\n              condition: elifCond,\n              body: elifBo